Q: How can I check if character in a string is a letter? (Python) A: <pre><code>def recursive_min_max(seq, index=0):
    if index == len(seq) - 1:
        return seq[index], seq[index]

    minimum, maximum = recursive_min_max(seq, index + 1)

    if seq[index] &lt; minimum:
        minimum = seq[index]

    if seq[index] &gt; maximum:
        maximum = seq[index]

    return minimum, maximum


items = []

while True:
    value = input(&quot;Enter a value (#END to stop): &quot;)

    if value == &quot;#END&quot;:
        break

    items.append(float(value))

if len(items) == 0:
    print(&quot;No values entered.&quot;)
else:
    minimum, maximum = recursive_min_max(items)
    print(&quot;Minimum:&quot;, minimum)
    print(&quot;Maximum:&quot;, maximum)
</code></pre>

Q: URL Redirect with Javascript A: <p>If you're using a recent browser then it's possible to use the <a href="https://developer.mozilla.org/en-US/docs/Web/API/URL_Pattern_API" rel="nofollow noreferrer">URL Pattern API</a> (considered baseline since September 2025).</p>
<p>This allows you to match urls to specified patterns.</p>
<p>Here is an example that defines a pattern that matches your example &quot;old URL&quot;.</p>
<pre><code>// Regexp validations defined:
// - `username` must start with '@'
// - `id` must be one or more digits
const pattern = new URLPattern({pathname: '/:username(@.+)/files/:id(\\d+)'});

function rewrite(oldUrl){
  let newUrl = null;
  const urlResult = pattern.exec(oldUrl);
  if (urlResult){
    const groups = urlResult.pathname.groups;
    newUrl = `https://www.newsite.com/path/path2/${groups.username}/${groups.id}`;
  }

  return newUrl;
}
</code></pre>
<p>In this example, any urls which don't match the pattern will return <code>null</code>.</p>
<pre><code>rewrite('https://www.oldsite.com/@username/files/12345');
// 'https://www.newsite.com/path/path2/@username/12345'

rewrite('https://www.oldsite.com/username/files/12345');
// null
</code></pre>

